<Base lang="en-US" pageType="CollectionPage" title="Martín Fierro for Students: Canto-by-Canto Study Guides" description="Study Martín Fierro in English with canto summaries, original Spanish excerpts, vocabulary, themes, characters and comprehension questions.">
<main class="students"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English edition</a> / <a href="/en/library/">Popular library</a> / Study guides</nav><header><p class="eyebrow">OPEN STUDY LIBRARY · ENGLISH STUDY RESOURCES</p><h1>Martín Fierro <em>for Students</em></h1><p class="intro">Learn the story canto by canto without losing contact with José Hernández’s original Spanish words. Each published guide includes the plot, major voices, selected authentic verses, literary themes and questions for reflection.</p></header><section><h2>Part One — El gaucho Martín Fierro (1872)</h2><div class="cards">{lessons.map(x=><article><p class="chapter">CANTO {x.n}</p><h3><a href={`/en/students/canto-${x.n}/`}>{x.title}</a></h3><p>{x.desc}</p><a href={`/en/students/canto-${x.n}/`}>Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href={`/leer/ida/${x.n}/`} lang="es">Original poem</a></article>)}</div><p class="note">All thirteen cantos of the 1872 first part now have individual study guides. Additional cantos will be linked only when their guides have been completed and verified against the original corpus.</p></section><section><h2>Part Two — La vuelta de Martín Fierro (1879)</h2><div class="cards"><article><p class="chapter">CANTO I</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-1/">The Return Begins: Fierro Sings Again</a></h3><p>The narrator resumes his song and claims a right to be heard.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-1/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/1/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO II</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-2/">Fierro and Cruz Arrive Beyond the Frontier</a></h3><p>Arrival at an Indigenous settlement, suspicion and captivity.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-2/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/2/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO III</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-3/">Two Years Apart: Fierro and Cruz Survive Captivity</a></h3><p>Separation, shelter and hunting for survival.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-3/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/3/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO IV</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-4/">Horsemen, Raids and Fierro’s Prejudices</a></h3><p>Riding skills, raid descriptions and racist stereotypes.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-4/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/4/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO V</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-5/">The Spoils of Raids and Women’s Labor</a></h3><p>Contradictions in Fierro’s descriptions of life beyond the frontier.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-5/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/5/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO VI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-6/">Epidemic and the Death of Cruz</a></h3><p>Illness, gratitude, friendship and Cruz’s last request.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-6/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/6/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO VII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-7/">Mourning Cruz and Finding a Captive Woman</a></h3><p>Fierro buries his friend and finds a wounded woman.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-7/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/7/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO VIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-8/">The Captive Woman and the Murder of Her Child</a></h3><p>Captivity, violence and the narrator’s testimony.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-8/">Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/8/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO IX</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-9/">Fierro Defends the Captive Woman</a></h3><p>The captive woman saves Fierro during a deadly fight.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-9/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/9/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO X</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-10/">Escape Across the Pampas</a></h3><p>Horse training, navigation, flight and a difficult return.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-10/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/10/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-11/">Fierro Reunites with His Sons</a></h3><p>Reunion, the death of his wife and memories of earlier violence.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-11/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/11/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-12/">A Son's Orphanhood and Prison</a></h3><p>A son recounts mistreatment, solitary confinement and the value of speech.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-12/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/12/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-13/">The Younger Son and His Inheritance</a></h3><p>Care from an aunt, inheritance and the appointment of a guardian.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-13/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/13/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XIV</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-14/">Old Vizcacha as Guardian</a></h3><p>The younger son describes neglect, theft and an abusive guardian.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-14/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/14/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XV</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-15/">Old Vizcacha's Advice</a></h3><p>Survival, the judge and self-interest; compare with Fierro's later counsel.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-15/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/15/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XVI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-16/">Old Vizcacha Falls Ill and Dies</a></h3><p>A folk healer, audience corrections and the guardian's final days.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-16/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/16/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XVII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-17/">After Vizcacha's Death</a></h3><p>Stories about the deceased, the inventory and the boy's lost inheritance.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-17/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/17/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XVIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-18/">After Vizcacha's Death</a></h3><p>The younger son mourns and abandons the dwelling.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-18/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/18/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XIX</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-19/">Love, a Diviner and Frontier Recruitment</a></h3><p>An unrequited love and another forced move to the frontier.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-19/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/19/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XX</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-20/">Picardía Arrives at the Reunion</a></h3><p>A new singer enters, but his life story has not yet begun.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-20/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/20/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-21/">Picardía's Childhood</a></h3><p>Loss, child labor and the humorous prayer episodes.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-21/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/21/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-22/">Picardía's Gambling Years</a></h3><p>Deception at card games and a warning against gambling.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-22/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/22/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-23/">Picardía and the Police Officer</a></h3><p>Rigged winnings, abuse of authority and a bitter feud.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-23/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/23/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXIV</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-24/">Picardía Defends His Vote</a></h3><p>An election, police punishment and political coercion.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-24/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/24/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXV</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-25/">Forced Frontier Recruitment</a></h3><p>Men are rounded up while their families plead for them.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-25/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/25/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXVI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-26/">Picardía Discovers His Father</a></h3><p>Picardía learns he is the son of Sergeant Cruz.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-26/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/26/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXVII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-27/">Unpaid Frontier Service</a></h3><p>Picardía protests harsh military service and unpaid wages.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-27/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/27/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXVIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-28/">Where the Soldiers' Rations Go</a></h3><p>Alleged corruption and shortages within the military hierarchy.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-28/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/28/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXIX</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-29/">A Singer Challenges Martín Fierro</a></h3><p>A Black singer challenges Fierro; the contest begins later.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-29/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/29/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXX</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-30/">The Moreno and Fierro: Singing Contest</a></h3><p>Poetic questions, racial equality, law and a reckoning with the past.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-30/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/30/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXXI</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-31/">A Fight Prevented; A Family Separates</a></h3><p>The crowd prevents conflict and Fierro prepares his advice.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-31/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/31/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXXII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-32/">Martín Fierro's Advice to His Sons</a></h3><p>Brotherhood, honest work, nonviolence, and a comparison with Vizcacha.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-32/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/32/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article><article><p class="chapter">CANTO XXXIII</p><h3><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-33/">Final Canto: New Names, Gaucho Rights and Farewell</a></h3><p>The four companions separate; the poet closes with an appeal for gaucho rights.</p><a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-33/">Read the study guide</a> · <a href="/leer/vuelta/33/" lang="es">Original poem</a></article></div><p class="note">All thirty-three cantos of the 1879 sequel now have verified English study guides. Together with the thirteen cantos of Part One, the complete collection covers all forty-six cantos.</p></section><section class="feature"><h2>Read across the whole poem</h2><p><a href="/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/">Old Vizcacha versus Martín Fierro</a> compares different approaches to life and morality using original passages from the sequel’s Cantos XV and XXXII. The <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">complete advice to his sons</a> is also available with English explanations.</p><p><a href="/en/martin-fierro-summary/">Story summary</a> · <a href="/en/characters/">Main characters</a> · <a href="/en/library/themes-and-analysis/">Themes and analysis</a> · <a href="/en/magazine/frontier-forts/">Frontier history</a></p></section></main>
